What this handbook is not
To stay deep where it matters, the book stays narrow on purpose. It is not a comprehensive textbook of AI and machine learning (ML): it leaves out computer vision (machines that interpret images) and general natural-language processing (machines that handle text), keeping only the representation learning — turning words and items into vectors — that recommenders actually use. Within recommendation it follows one through-line — collaborative filtering, from its classical form to graph- and LLM-based models — and sorts the rest of the field into three bins:

Covered in full — a part, a chapter or a full section each:

sequential / session-based recommendation (Part IV; the models introduced above);
training and serving (Part VII): negative sampling (picking items a user did not click, for the model to rank below the ones they did), the training loop, and the retrieval-then-ranking funnel;
factorization machines (Traditional Recommender Systems §8): the bridge from matrix factorization to models that use many features of a user and an item;
deep click-through-rate models (Part VIII): Wide & Deep (a linear part plus a deep network), DeepFM (a deep network plus a factorization machine) and the Deep & Cross Network (DCN), neural networks that learn which combinations of features predict a click — the workhorse of the ranking stage;
bandits (Part VIII): the explore–exploit balance — try something new, or show a proven favorite? — with the Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) rule and Thompson sampling as two ways to strike it;
generative recommendation, inside Part VI: the model writes out an item’s semantic ID (identifier), a short code in which similar items share a prefix, using codes learned by a network called an RQ-VAE (residual-quantized variational autoencoder);
bias, fairness and feedback loops (Evaluation Metrics §12): how skewed data, and a recommender’s own past picks, can tilt what it shows.

A short pointer appendix each — the framing and the key works, not a full course: reinforcement learning and federated / privacy-preserving recommendation (glossed with the appendices above).
Out of scope as fields of their own — where the book’s path crosses one of them, it teaches only the tool that step needs and points onward:

causal / counterfactual recommendation: what would have happened had the system shown something else (met only through its first tools, such as inverse propensity scoring, which reweights each logged click by how unlikely it was to be shown: Evaluation Metrics §12.4, Bandits & Online Recommendation §7);
context-aware recommendation, which uses the situation, such as time or place;
conversational recommendation, a dialogue with the user (met where an LLM holds the dialogue itself: Large Language Models and Recommendation §2.4);
knowledge-graph methods: a web of facts about items, such as who directed what;
multi-stakeholder settings: balancing users, sellers and the platform (met as fairness to the sellers and creators whose items are shown: Evaluation Metrics §12.5);
large-scale machine-learning operations (MLOps): keeping models running reliably in production.


A focused ladder you can finish beats an encyclopedia you cannot.

a state \(s\) — a summary of the situation now (the user’s recent history, time, context);
an action \(a\) — what the agent (the recommender) does (which item, or slate (a page of items), to show);
a reward \(r\) — the immediate payoff (a click, a watch-minute, a return visit);
a transition — taking action \(a\) in state \(s\) moves you to a new state \(s'\), possibly random (drawn from a distribution), and in an MDP actions can change the next state;
a discount factor \(\gamma\) (below): how much a later reward is worth.





Worked example — a discounted return. Suppose over three steps the agent collects rewards \(r = (1,\,0,\,1)\) with discount \(\gamma = 0.9\). Then \(G = 1 + 0.9(0) + 0.9^2(1) = 1 + 0 + 0.81 = 1.81.\) The click two steps out is worth only \(\gamma^2 = 0.81\) of an immediate one — the same click, discounted for being later. Push \(\gamma\to 0\) and the objective collapses to a bandit-like myopic “maximize the immediate reward”; push \(\gamma\to 1\) and the far future matters almost as much as now: a reward ten steps out keeps \(0.9^{10}=0.349\) of its worth at \(\gamma=0.9\) but \(0.99^{10}=0.904\) at \(\gamma=0.99\). (The figure below draws the three steps.)







Figure D.1: The discounted return \(G=\sum_t\gamma^t r_t\) on the §1 worked example (\(r=(1,0,1)\), \(\gamma=0.9\)). Each bar is one step’s discounted reward \(\gamma^t r_t\): the immediate \(r_0\) counts in full, but the identical reward at \(t{=}2\) is shrunk to \(\gamma^2=0.81\). The bars sum to \(G=1.81\). Send \(\gamma\to0\) and only the first bar survives (a myopic, bandit-like objective); send \(\gamma\to1\) and every bar counts equally.






Worked example — long-term reward changes the choice. A user is in one state with two actions. Clickbait pays \(r=1\) now and the user then leaves (\(0\) next); quality pays \(0.5\) now and the user stays for a reward of \(1\) next (these later rewards are given, not modeled further). With \(\gamma=0.9\), \(Q(\text{clickbait})=1+0.9(0)=1.0\) and \(Q(\text{quality})=0.5+0.9(1)=1.4\), so the greedy policy shows quality and its value is \(V(s)=\max_a Q(s,a)=1.4\) (a policy that shows each action half the time has \(V=\tfrac12\cdot1.0+\tfrac12\cdot1.4=1.2\): \(V\) depends on the policy). A bandit, which sees only the first reward, picks clickbait (\(1&gt;0.5\)); at \(\gamma=0\) the two are \(1.0\) and \(0.5\), exactly the bandit’s choice. Quality wins whenever \(0.5+\gamma&gt;1\), that is \(\gamma&gt;0.5\).








Figure D.2: The reinforcement-learning loop. The agent picks an action from its policy; the environment (the user) returns a reward and a new state. What bandits lack is an action that can change the new state; because it can, the agent must optimize the discounted return \(G=\sum_t\gamma^t r_t\), not just the next reward. A bandit is this loop in which the action never changes the next state.

the server broadcasts the current global model \(w\) to a sample of client devices;
each client trains \(w\) for a few steps on its own local data (which never leaves the device), producing a local update;
each client sends back only its update — what it changed, \(\Delta_k=w_k-w\) (after a single local step \(\Delta_k=-\eta g_k\), with \(\eta\) the learning rate and \(g_k\) the gradient on that client’s data; the federated matrix factorization of §3 sends gradients) — and its example count \(n_k\), never raw interactions;
the server aggregates the updates into a new global \(w\), and the loop repeats.








Figure E.1: The federated-learning loop. The server broadcasts the global model \(w\) to client devices; each client trains on its own data \(D_k\) (which never leaves the device) and returns only a parameter update \(\Delta_k\) (with its example count \(n_k\)); the server combines them by FedAvg (§2). Across rounds a single shared model improves while the raw interactions stay distributed.
